# (#1361) the AOT's inlined num_guard (aot_num_guard_inl, the one every
# num_guard in aot_rt.h and the emitted C resolves to). A NaN collapses to 0
# on the soft path; under strict the VM raises "arithmetic: result is not a
# number". The NaN comes from the one raw non-finite a numeric context can
# read: a matmul that overflowed to inf (t296's raw element read), so
# inf - inf. Scalar arithmetic and the tensor kernel's elementwise add
# (aot_tensor_add) both take the guard.
nb is buffer of 4
nb[0] is 1e300
nb[1] is 1e300
nb[2] is 1e300
nb[3] is 1e300
nm is reshape of [nb, 2, 2]
np is matmul of [nm, nm]
mb is buffer of 4
mb[0] is -1e300
mb[1] is -1e300
mb[2] is -1e300
mb[3] is -1e300
mm is reshape of [mb, 2, 2]
nq is matmul of [mm, nm]
print of (np[0] > 1e308)
print of (nq[0] < -1e308)
try:
    d is np[0] - np[1]
    print of d
catch e:
    print of e.kind
    print of e.message
    print of e.line
try:
    s is add of [np, nq]
    print of s[0]
catch e:
    print of e.message
    print of e.line
print of (np[2] - 1)
print of "after"
